From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 20:20:17 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] fix(resources): keep resource image codecs in memory

Resource image bytes already reside in memory, but the ImageIO stream convenience methods can spool them to temporary files. An unavailable cache can prevent the bundled default skin from decoding and abort login, or stop PNG export.

Use memory-backed input and output streams for Content images without changing global ImageIO cache settings. Respect ImageIO input ownership and retain the original PNG passthrough. Test pixel decoding and both PNG export paths with an unwritable disk cache.

Read the image header with the same in-memory reader before decoding.
A few compressed PNG or JPEG bytes can declare dimensions that make
ImageIO allocate gigabytes, for example while item model conversion
copies pack textures. The native 1.26.60.5 client initializes libpng's
default 1,000,000-pixel side limits (store at 0x14e985fb7, IHDR check at
0x14e987000), and no limit setter appears in its libpng code, so a fixed
low bound would reject textures that native loads and kick the player
when conversion fails. Bound the decoded four-byte pixels by an eighth
of the heap instead, and by the largest Java array. Conversion holds a
decoded image and usually one ARGB copy on each of two converter
threads, so accepted images stay within half of the heap. A 2 GiB heap
accepts 8192 x 8192 textures. Regressions cover the heap scaling and a
header past the array bound that is rejected without decoding.

Repair a PNG whose zlib Adler-32 trailer is wrong and decode it again.
libpng 1.6 reports an inflate error that occurs after the last row as a
benign error, which read structs downgrade to a warning, and stb_image
does not check the trailer at all. Bedrock and the Java client therefore
load such textures, while ImageIO throws and aborted the whole pack
conversion, which kicked the player. The repair inflates the IDAT data
without the zlib wrapper, counts at most the bytes the IHDR allows, and
writes one IDAT with the recomputed checksum. Regressions decode a
texture with a wrong checksum.

Report an image that cannot be decoded, even after the checksum repair,
as no image. libpng also rejects image data that is corrupt or ends
before the last row, and Bedrock then draws that texture as missing and
keeps the rest of the pack. Throwing instead failed the whole pack
conversion and disconnected the player. The decoding size limit still
throws, because it protects the heap rather than reporting bad data.
Regressions report no image for an invalid deflate block and for a
stream that ends before the last row.

